WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.727
Coding Exercise 6 brings most of the module together in one priority rule.

00:00:05.727 --> 00:00:09.987
The highest-priority outcome is Do not contact.

00:00:09.987 --> 00:00:15.362
That applies when the record is inactive or consent is explicitly false.

00:00:15.362 --> 00:00:20.882
Put that branch first because it should override the lower-priority outcomes.

00:00:20.882 --> 00:00:27.157
Next comes Urgent. The patient must be active, consent must be explicitly true, severity must be High,

00:00:27.157 --> 00:00:31.762
and there must be no usable email address.

00:00:31.762 --> 00:00:39.342
Then comes Routine for active patients with explicit consent who did not already qualify as Urgent.

00:00:39.342 --> 00:00:41.657
Everything else becomes Review. Branch order is essential.

00:00:41.657 --> 00:00:49.627
If Routine is placed before Urgent, every active consenting patient will match Routine before PostgreSQL ever

00:00:49.627 --> 00:00:56.112
reaches the Urgent branch. The email check must also be NULL-aware.

00:00:56.112 --> 00:01:02.657
A blank or whitespace-only email should count as unavailable, not as valid contact information.

00:01:02.657 --> 00:01:06.782
When you finish, test one example from each category.

00:01:06.782 --> 00:01:12.454
That is how you validate a multi-branch decision rule instead of trusting the syntax alone.
